Bounded-Uncertainty Decision Theory (BUDT): A Formal Framework for Resource-Constrained Agency

---

Author: ouadi Maakoul 

Abstract

We introduce Bounded-Uncertainty Decision Theory (BUDT), a mathematical framework that formalises intelligence as a constrained stochastic control problem over a joint state space comprising latent environment dynamics, compressed belief representations, and evolving computational resources. We propose five axioms that capture the essential limitations faced by any physically embodied or computationally bounded agent: partial observability, finite memory, bounded computation, resource dynamics, and economically weighted utility. From these axioms we derive a resource-augmented partially observable Markov decision process (RC-POMDP) and define the Bounded-Resource Bellman Operator \mathcal{T}^{\mathcal{B}}. We prove that under mild regularity conditions \mathcal{T}^{\mathcal{B}} is a contraction mapping in a weighted sup-norm, guaranteeing the existence of a unique optimal value function and a stationary optimal policy. The theory unifies several major subfields of artificial intelligence—including reinforcement learning, continual learning, automated machine learning, and real-time systems—as limiting cases or special parameterisations of the BUDT objective. The central claim of the thesis is that intelligence is the contraction fixed point of decision-making under bounded belief, bounded memory, and bounded computation. This document provides the complete axiomatic foundation, mathematical structure, and core theorems of BUDT, establishing it as a novel decision theory for resource-constrained agency.

---

1. Introduction and Motivation

The study of intelligent behaviour has traditionally been divided among disparate mathematical frameworks. Reinforcement learning (RL) models the maximisation of cumulative reward in a Markovian environment, typically assuming unbounded computational capacity and perfect memory. Bayesian inference provides a normative theory for belief updating under uncertainty but ignores the cost of performing inference. Information theory quantifies the limits of communication and compression but does not prescribe how to act. Real-time systems engineering imposes hard deadlines on computation but abstracts away from statistical uncertainty.

Yet any physically realised intelligent agent—whether a biological organism, a robot, or a large language model deployed on a GPU cluster—operates under a unified set of constraints: it never knows the true state of the world with certainty; it cannot store or process infinite information; and its computational resources are finite and fluctuate over time. The central premise of this thesis is that these constraints are not incidental complications but are definitional to intelligence. An adequate mathematical theory of intelligence must therefore treat resource limitations and uncertainty as first-class citizens.

Bounded-Uncertainty Decision Theory (BUDT) provides such a theory. It integrates three classical pillars—stochastic control, information theory, and constrained optimisation—into a single coherent framework. The theory does not propose a new algorithm; it proposes a normative definition of what it means to act intelligently under real-world limitations. From this definition, algorithms and architectures can be derived systematically.

This chapter presents the complete axiomatic and mathematical core of BUDT. It is structured as follows. Section 2 states the five foundational axioms. Section 3 constructs the formal stochastic control system. Section 4 defines the BUDT objective function. Section 5 introduces the Bounded-Resource Bellman Operator and proves its contraction property. Section 6 establishes the existence and uniqueness of the optimal value function and policy. Section 7 demonstrates the unification of classical AI subfields as special cases of BUDT. Section 8 discusses the philosophical interpretation of the theory, and Section 9 outlines the path from theory to algorithm.

