2. The Axioms of Bounded Intelligence

We begin by stating the five axioms that define the BUDT agent. These axioms are not derived; they are posited as the minimal set of constraints that characterise physically realisable intelligent systems.

Axiom A1 — Bounded State Access (Partial Observability and Belief Compression)

The agent never directly observes the true latent state s_t \in \mathcal{S} of the environment. Instead, it maintains a compressed belief state b_t \in \mathcal{B}, which is a probability distribution over \mathcal{S} or a parametric approximation thereof, computed from a lossy memory representation m_t \in \mathcal{M}:

b_t = \mathcal{B}(m_t),

where \mathcal{B}: \mathcal{M} \to \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{S}) is a measurable mapping from the memory space to the space of probability measures on \mathcal{S}. The memory m_t itself is a finite-capacity summary of the observation-action history.

Interpretation: Intelligence is fundamentally information-limited inference. The agent must decide under irreducible uncertainty, and the precision of its beliefs is constrained by the capacity of its memory system.

Axiom A2 — Bounded Computation (Costly Decision-Making)

Every action a_t \in \mathcal{A} incurs a measurable computational cost \mathcal{C}(a_t, m_t) \in \mathbb{R}^K_+. The cost is a function of the action selected and the current memory state (which determines the complexity of the inference required to select that action). The agent possesses a finite resource budget \rho_t \in \mathcal{R} \subset \mathbb{R}^K_+ and may only select actions whose cost does not exceed this budget:

\mathcal{C}(a_t, m_t) \leq \rho_t \quad \text{(component-wise)}.

Interpretation: Computation is not an external overhead; it is an intrinsic part of the decision dynamics. The cost functional \mathcal{C} can capture FLOPs, wall-clock time, energy consumption, or any other measurable resource expenditure.

Axiom A3 — Bounded Resources as a Markov Process

The resource vector \rho_t evolves stochastically over time according to a controlled Markov process:

\rho_{t+1} = \Pi_{\mathcal{R}}\Big( \rho_t - \mathcal{C}(a_t, m_t) + \epsilon_t \Big),

where \epsilon_t is an exogenous recharge (or depletion) noise with known distribution \nu, and \Pi_{\mathcal{R}} is the Euclidean projection onto the compact convex set \mathcal{R} of feasible resource states.

Interpretation: "Thinking time", energy, and memory bandwidth are state variables of the world. They are depleted by action selection and replenished by rest or external supply. The agent must manage its resource state as carefully as it manages its physical state.

Axiom A4 — Utility under Constraint (Economic Rationality)

The agent does not maximise raw reward alone. It maximises resource-adjusted utility, which subtracts the opportunity cost of resource consumption:

\mathcal{J}(\pi) = \mathbb{E}_\pi \left[ \sum_{t=0}^\infty \gamma^t \Big( \mathcal{R}(s_t, a_t) - \lambda^\top \mathcal{C}(a_t, m_t) \Big) \right],

where \lambda \in \mathbb{R}^K_+ is a vector of Lagrange multipliers representing the shadow price of each resource.

Interpretation: Rationality is always economically weighted. An action is only worth taking if its expected reward exceeds the value of the resources it consumes. The multipliers \lambda encode the agent's "exchange rate" between utility and computational effort.

Axiom A5 — Compression Principle (Lossy Memory as Encoding)

The memory state m_t is a lossy compression of the belief state and resource state, with a strict bound on the mutual information between the true latent state and the memory:

m_t = \phi(b_t, \rho_t), \qquad \mathcal{I}(s_t; m_t) \leq C_m,

where \phi: \mathcal{B} \times \mathcal{R} \to \mathcal{M} is a measurable compression mapping, and C_m < \infty is the memory capacity.

Interpretation: Forgetting is not a failure of the agent; it is a constraint-satisfying encoding strategy. The agent must decide what information to retain and what to discard, trading off memory fidelity against storage cost.

