3. Formal System Definition: The Resource-Constrained POMDP

We now construct a rigorous stochastic control system that instantiates Axioms A1–A5.

3.1 The Augmented State Space

Let the underlying probability space be (\Omega, \mathcal{F}, \{\mathcal{F}_t\}_{t \ge 0}, \mathbb{P}), where \mathcal{F}_t is the filtration generated by all events up to time t. The augmented state at time t is the triple:

X_t = (s_t, b_t, \rho_t) \in \mathcal{X} = \mathcal{S} \times \mathcal{B} \times \mathcal{R},

where:

· \mathcal{S} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^{d_s} is the latent environment state space (a Polish space).
· \mathcal{B} = \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{S}) is the space of probability measures on \mathcal{S}, equipped with the topology of weak convergence and the Borel \sigma-algebra.
· \mathcal{R} \subset \mathbb{R}^K_+ is a compact convex set of feasible resource vectors.

The memory state m_t \in \mathcal{M} is a deterministic function of the belief and resources:

m_t = \phi(b_t, \rho_t),

where \phi: \mathcal{B} \times \mathcal{R} \to \mathcal{M} is a continuous compression mapping with bounded range, and \mathcal{M} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^{d_m} is a compact set with d_m \ll d_s.

3.2 The Joint Transition Kernel

The environment evolves according to a structured transition kernel P that factorises as follows:

Latent State Dynamics:

s_{t+1} \sim P_S(\cdot \mid s_t, a_t),

where P_S: \mathcal{S} \times \mathcal{A} \to \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{S}) is a Markov kernel, absolutely continuous with respect to a reference measure on \mathcal{S}.

Observation Model:

o_{t+1} \sim P_O(\cdot \mid s_{t+1}),

where P_O: \mathcal{S} \to \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{O}) is the observation kernel. The observation space \mathcal{O} is a Polish space.

Belief Update (Variational Bayesian Filter):

Given the current belief b_t, action a_t, and new observation o_{t+1}, the exact Bayesian posterior is:

\tilde{b}_{t+1}(ds) = \frac{P_O(o_{t+1} \mid s) \int_{\mathcal{S}} P_S(s \mid s', a_t) \, b_t(ds')}{\int_{\mathcal{S}} P_O(o_{t+1} \mid s'') \int_{\mathcal{S}} P_S(s'' \mid s', a_t) \, b_t(ds') \, ds''}.

This posterior is generally intractable to represent exactly. The agent therefore computes a compressed belief b_{t+1} by solving a variational optimisation problem:

b_{t+1} = \arg\min_{q \in \mathcal{Q}} \Big\{ \mathcal{D}(q \parallel \tilde{b}_{t+1}) + \lambda_{\text{info}} \cdot \mathcal{I}(q; m_t) \Big\},

where:

· \mathcal{Q} \subset \mathcal{B} is a feasible set of parametric distributions with bounded complexity (e.g., Gaussian, mixture of N components, or particle set of size N).
· \mathcal{D} is a statistical divergence (e.g., Kullback-Leibler divergence or Wasserstein distance).
· \mathcal{I}(q; m_t) is a mutual information term that penalises beliefs that require large changes to the memory representation.
· \lambda_{\text{info}} \ge 0 is a regularisation parameter controlling the compression strength.

This defines a measurable belief update operator \mathcal{B}: \mathcal{B} \times \mathcal{A} \times \mathcal{O} \to \mathcal{B}:

b_{t+1} = \mathcal{B}(b_t, a_t, o_{t+1}).

Resource Dynamics:

The resource vector evolves according to:

\rho_{t+1} = \Pi_{\mathcal{R}}\Big( \rho_t - \mathcal{C}(a_t, m_t) + \epsilon_t \Big),

with:

· \mathcal{C}: \mathcal{A} \times \mathcal{M} \to \mathbb{R}^K_+ a continuous cost functional.
· \epsilon_t \overset{\text{i.i.d.}}{\sim} \nu, where \nu is a probability measure on \mathbb{R}^K with compact support and mean \mu_\epsilon \ge 0 (component-wise).
· \Pi_{\mathcal{R}} the Euclidean projection onto \mathcal{R}.

Memory Update:

m_{t+1} = \phi(b_{t+1}, \rho_{t+1}).

The full augmented state transition is therefore given by:

P(X_{t+1} \in dx' \mid X_t = x, a_t = a) = \int_{\mathcal{O}} \delta_{s'}(ds) \delta_{b'}(db) \delta_{\rho'}(d\rho) \, P_O(do \mid s') P_S(ds' \mid s, a) \nu(d\epsilon),

where b' = \mathcal{B}(b, a, o) and \rho' = \Pi_{\mathcal{R}}(\rho - \mathcal{C}(a, \phi(b,\rho)) + \epsilon).

3.3 The Resource-Constrained Policy Space

A decision rule is a measurable mapping \pi: \mathcal{M} \times \mathcal{R} \to \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{A}). Given the current memory m and resources \rho, the agent samples an action from \pi(\cdot \mid m, \rho).

Definition 1 (Feasible Action Set). For a given memory m \in \mathcal{M} and resource vector \rho \in \mathcal{R}, the feasible action set is:

\mathcal{A}_{\text{valid}}(\rho, m) = \{ a \in \mathcal{A} \mid \mathcal{C}(a, m) \leq \rho \ \text{component-wise} \}.

If \mathcal{A}_{\text{valid}}(\rho, m) = \emptyset, the agent must select a designated null action a_\emptyset \in \mathcal{A} with minimal cost (e.g., idle or wait).

Definition 2 (Admissible Policy Class \Pi_{\text{adm}}). A stationary policy \pi is admissible if:

1. Feasibility: \text{supp}(\pi(\cdot \mid m, \rho)) \subseteq \mathcal{A}_{\text{valid}}(\rho, m) for all (m, \rho) \in \mathcal{M} \times \mathcal{R}.
2. Reccurence: The induced Markov chain \{X_t\}_{t \ge 0} on the augmented state space is positive Harris recurrent on a subset of \mathcal{X} (ensuring the process does not become trapped in resource starvation).

The class of all admissible stationary policies is denoted \Pi_{\text{adm}}.

