7. Unification of AI Subfields

A central claim of BUDT is that it provides a unifying mathematical language for disparate areas of AI research. We now demonstrate how classical problems are obtained as special cases or limiting regimes of the BUDT framework.

7.1 Unconstrained Reinforcement Learning (POMDPs)

Consider the limit where resources are infinite (\mathcal{R} = \mathbb{R}^K_+) and the resource prices vanish (\lambda = 0). The cost functional \mathcal{C} becomes irrelevant, the constraint \mathcal{A}_{\text{valid}} = \mathcal{A} is vacuous, and the resource dynamics decouple. The objective reduces to:

\mathcal{J}(\pi) = \mathbb{E}_\pi \left[ \sum_{t=0}^\infty \gamma^t \mathcal{R}(s_t, a_t) \right],

and the belief update \mathcal{B} can be taken as exact Bayesian filtering (since memory capacity is unbounded). The Bounded-Resource Bellman Operator reduces to the standard POMDP Bellman operator. Thus:

Proposition 1. The standard POMDP is the limit of BUDT as \rho \to \infty and \lambda \to 0.

7.2 Continual Learning

In continual learning, an agent learns a sequence of tasks without catastrophic forgetting. Within BUDT, this corresponds to a regime where the memory capacity C_m is small, and the cost of updating the memory representation is high. Specifically, the variational belief update includes a strong regulariser \lambda_{\text{info}} \mathcal{I}(q; m_t) that penalises changes to the memory. The policy must balance performance on the current task against the preservation of knowledge encoded in m_t. The BUDT objective naturally yields algorithms that protect previous knowledge, as overwriting memory incurs a cost that must be justified by immediate reward gains.

Proposition 2. Continual learning is a BUDT instance with a high memory retention cost \lambda_{\text{info}} and a strong compression constraint C_m.

7.3 Automated Machine Learning (AutoML) and Neural Architecture Search (NAS)

In NAS, the goal is to select a neural network architecture \theta \in \Theta that maximises validation accuracy subject to a computational budget (e.g., FLOPs or latency). This fits exactly into BUDT with the following identifications:

· The action a_t is the choice of architecture (a one-shot decision or a sequence of architectural modifications).
· The reward \mathcal{R} is the validation accuracy.
· The cost functional \mathcal{C} measures the inference or training cost of the architecture.
· The resource \rho is the total computational budget.

The BUDT optimal policy \pi^* solves the budget-constrained architecture search problem. Moreover, the Lagrange multiplier \lambda has a direct economic interpretation as the marginal value of one additional FLOP.

Proposition 3. AutoML/NAS is a finite-horizon BUDT problem where the policy selects a discrete architecture under a hard resource budget.

7.4 Large Language Model Inference

Consider an LLM generating a sequence of tokens. The memory m_t is the key-value (KV) cache, which grows with the context length. The resource \rho_t includes the available GPU memory and the time budget per token. The cost functional \mathcal{C}(a_t, m_t) captures the FLOPs and memory access cost of generating the next token given the current cache. The constraint \mathcal{C} \le \rho_t enforces that the model must operate within its hardware limits. The BUDT framework explains phenomena such as attention pruning and early exiting as optimal strategies under resource pressure: the agent chooses to skip some attention heads or exit early to save computation when the expected information gain is low relative to the cost.

Proposition 4. LLM token generation is a real-time BUDT process where the policy selects the next token and optionally the compute path under strict memory and latency constraints.

7.5 Real-Time Systems and Robotics

In real-time control, the agent must compute an action within a fixed time window \tau. This is a hard constraint on the latency component of \mathcal{C}. BUDT provides a principled way to trade off decision quality against computation time: the agent can use a fast, approximate policy when time is scarce and a more accurate, expensive policy when time permits. The BUDT Bellman operator automatically balances this trade-off.

Proposition 5. Real-time decision making is a BUDT instance where one component of \rho is the time budget and the corresponding component of \mathcal{C} is the policy evaluation latency.

7.6 Multi-Agent Systems

The framework extends naturally to multi-agent settings by augmenting the state space with the states and beliefs of other agents. The resource constraints then apply per agent, and the cost functional can include communication costs between agents.

Proposition 6. Multi-agent systems are BUDT instances where the augmented state includes other agents' parameters, and \mathcal{C} includes communication costs.

