8. Philosophical Interpretation: Intelligence as Equilibrium

The core theorem of BUDT states that under axioms A1–A5, the optimal behaviour of an agent is given by the unique fixed point of the operator \mathcal{T}^{\mathcal{B}}. This has profound implications for our understanding of intelligence.

Traditional View: Intelligence is the ability to maximise a reward signal. This leads to an endless pursuit of optimisation, often resulting in algorithms that are computationally profligate and brittle.

BUDT View: Intelligence is the equilibrium of a constrained dynamical system. The agent does not seek an unattainable global maximum; it seeks a stable balance between reward, computation, memory, and uncertainty. The fixed point V^* represents the optimal trade-off given the agent's inherent limitations.

This re-frames intelligence as a bounded rationality concept in the tradition of Herbert Simon, but with a fully formalised mathematical foundation. The agent is satisficing in a precise, optimal sense: it cannot do better given its finite resources.

The Fundamental Insight:

\boxed{\text{Intelligence} = \text{Fixed Point of } \mathcal{T}^{\mathcal{B}}}

Not optimal search. Not perfect planning. Not infinite reasoning. But a stable, resource-aware equilibrium.

