9. From Theory to Algorithm: The Path Forward

The axiomatic core of BUDT is now complete. It defines what an intelligent agent should do. The next phase of the PhD is to derive how an agent can approximate this ideal in practice. This requires the development of a BUDT Solver Engine.

The main challenges for algorithmic instantiation are:

1. Belief Representation: Exact Bayesian filtering is intractable. Practical implementations will use particle filters, variational autoencoders, or recurrent neural networks to learn the compression mapping \phi and the belief decoder b(\cdot \mid m).
2. Cost Functional Estimation: The cost \mathcal{C}(a, m) must be measurable on the target hardware. This may involve profiling the inference latency and energy consumption of the policy network.
3. Constrained Optimisation: The maximisation over \mathcal{A}_{\text{valid}}(\rho, m) must be solved online. For discrete action spaces, this is a knapsack-constrained argmax. For continuous actions, it may involve projection steps.
4. Value Function Approximation: The value function V(m, \rho) lives in a high-dimensional space. Deep neural networks can be used to approximate V, trained via temporal difference learning with the BUDT Bellman target.

A concrete algorithmic sketch is as follows:

Algorithm: BUDT Actor-Critic

· Input: Resource prices \lambda, capacity C_m, cost functional \mathcal{C}.
· Initialise: Parametric belief encoder \phi_\theta, policy \pi_\psi, value network V_\chi.
· Loop:
  1. Observe o_t, update belief b_t = \text{Encoder}(o_t, m_{t-1}).
  2. Compute memory m_t = \phi_\theta(b_t, \rho_t).
  3. Determine feasible actions \mathcal{A}_{\text{valid}}(\rho_t, m_t).
  4. Sample action a_t \sim \pi_\psi(\cdot \mid m_t, \rho_t) restricted to feasible set.
  5. Execute a_t, measure reward r_t and actual cost c_t.
  6. Update resource state \rho_{t+1} = \rho_t - c_t + \epsilon_t.
  7. Compute TD target: y_t = r_t - \lambda^\top c_t + \gamma V_\chi(m_{t+1}, \rho_{t+1}).
  8. Update critic: \chi \leftarrow \chi - \alpha \nabla_\chi (V_\chi(m_t, \rho_t) - y_t)^2.
  9. Update actor: \psi \leftarrow \psi + \beta \nabla_\psi \log \pi_\psi(a_t \mid m_t, \rho_t) (y_t - V_\chi(m_t, \rho_t)).

This algorithm is a direct implementation of the BUDT fixed-point logic and can be instantiated for any of the unified subproblems described in Section 7.

---

10. Conclusion

We have presented the complete axiomatic and mathematical core of Bounded-Uncertainty Decision Theory. The framework rests on five axioms that capture the essential limitations of physically embodied intelligence: partial observability, bounded computation, evolving resources, economically weighted utility, and lossy memory compression. From these axioms we constructed a resource-constrained POMDP, defined the Bounded-Resource Bellman Operator \mathcal{T}^{\mathcal{B}}, and proved that it is a contraction in a weighted sup-norm. This guarantees the existence and uniqueness of an optimal value function and an optimal stationary policy. The theory unifies classical AI subfields as special cases or limiting regimes, and it re-frames intelligence as the equilibrium fixed point of a constrained stochastic control system.

The next phase of the research will focus on algorithmic realisation, empirical validation, and the derivation of finite-sample regret bounds. BUDT provides a rigorous foundation for building AI systems that are not only performant but also efficient, robust, and aligned with the resource realities of the physical world.

